# Routing RECALL/PRECISION over a gold SET — run-routing-recall.py
#
# SELECTION RULE (declared so the metric is not circular). Each gold set is either:
#   "router"  — transcribed from the router's own Cross-cutting routing rules in
#               skills/sota/SKILL.md, which state the composition in prose. The eval
#               then asks whether the DESCRIPTIONS deliver what the router BODY
#               promises. Those two surfaces are written at different times by
#               different hands and nothing checks they agree.
#   "derived" — no stated composition exists; the reasoning is in `why`. These are the
#               contested shapes from two external assessments (2026-09-06) that claimed
#               a missing skill where the content is actually spread across several.
#   "control" — a task whose honest answer is ONE skill. Present to detect
#               over-selection: a model that loads five skills here is padding context.
#
# MEASUREMENT vs REGRESSION: this set is a MEASUREMENT instrument (what is routing
# recall today?), not a regression pin. Cases are not selected by model performance —
# they were written before the first run, from the router text and the two assessments.
# A mis-route found here earns a pinned case in desc-routing-regressions.jsonl.
{"id": "mcp_server_audit", "gold_source": "derived", "why": "Two assessments called MCP security a missing skill. The content exists in three places at once — code-security rules/08 (tool poisoning, rug pulls, MCP03:2025), sandboxing rules/05 (agent execution + egress), threat-modeling rules/03 (tool shadowing). If routing cannot assemble them, the gap is reachability, not coverage.", "task": "Audit the MCP server we expose to third-party agents: tool descriptions, the authorization boundary on each tool, and what a malicious server in the same session could do.", "expect": ["sota-code-security", "sota-sandboxing", "sota-threat-modeling"]}
{"id": "k8s_cluster_audit", "gold_source": "router", "why": "Router cross-cutting rule 9 states this composition verbatim: 'A K8s cluster audit loads sota-kubernetes + sota-network-security + sota-sandboxing'.", "task": "Do a security audit of our Kubernetes cluster: RBAC and escalation paths, admission control, what a compromised pod can reach, and whether workloads are isolated from each other.", "expect": ["sota-kubernetes", "sota-network-security", "sota-sandboxing"]}
{"id": "next_security_review", "gold_source": "router", "why": "Router rule 6 states 'A React/Next or Vue/Nuxt security review pulls all four'.", "task": "Security-review our Next.js app: the Server Action boundary, what leaks from server to client components, hydration, and the CSS/accessibility regressions the last redesign introduced.", "expect": ["sota-frontend-design", "sota-web-frameworks", "sota-javascript-typescript", "sota-performance"]}
{"id": "untrusted_feed_ingest", "gold_source": "router", "why": "Router rule 13: ingesting untrusted/attacker-authored data -> code-security rules/09, with sandboxing rules/04 for parser isolation.", "task": "We ingest third-party RSS feeds and user-uploaded archives and parse them server-side. Harden that path.", "expect": ["sota-code-security", "sota-sandboxing"]}
{"id": "evidence_shell_script", "gold_source": "router", "why": "Router rule 17: a script that produces or verifies evidence is a security control written in shell — add code-security rules/10 and rules/12/15.", "task": "Review deploy-attest.sh, the script that writes our build attestation ledger and verifies it before release.", "expect": ["sota-shell-scripting", "sota-code-security"]}
{"id": "crypto_key_rotation", "gold_source": "router", "why": "Router rule 18: algorithm/AEAD choice -> code-security rules/04; the key MATERIAL, storage and rotation -> secrets-management; TLS/PKI transport -> network-security rules/06.", "task": "We need to rotate the signing keys for our API tokens without downtime, and move them out of environment variables.", "expect": ["sota-code-security", "sota-secrets-management"]}
{"id": "agent_subagent_delegation", "gold_source": "derived", "why": "An assessment claimed agent-engineering is a missing layer. llm-engineering rules/04 covers agents/tools; sandboxing rules/05 covers executing model output. Does routing assemble them for a delegation-shaped task?", "task": "Our coding agent spawns subagents that sometimes duplicate each other's work and occasionally run shell commands we did not intend. Fix the delegation and tool-permission design.", "expect": ["sota-llm-engineering", "sota-sandboxing"]}
{"id": "browser_verification", "gold_source": "derived", "why": "An assessment claimed browser/UI verification is a missing discipline. testing rules/05 owns e2e/selectors/flake; frontend-design owns a11y and visual craft. Reachability test.", "task": "Before we ship, verify in a real browser that the checkout flow works end to end and that nothing regressed visually or in accessibility.", "expect": ["sota-testing", "sota-frontend-design"]}
{"id": "single_go_error_control", "gold_source": "control", "why": "Over-selection control. The honest answer is one language skill; a model that also loads testing, security and architecture here is padding context, which is invisible to a top-1 metric.", "task": "Rewrite this Go function's error handling to wrap with %w and stop swallowing the sentinel.", "expect": ["sota-golang"]}
{"id": "single_ux_copy_control", "gold_source": "control", "why": "Second over-selection control, in a domain with three adjacent word-shaped skills (ux-writing / copywriting / docs-workflow) that a keyword matcher confuses.", "task": "Reword the error message shown when a password reset link has expired.", "expect": ["sota-ux-writing"]}
